    <paragraph>For the following functions $$f(x),$$ determine whether there is an asymptote at $$x = a.$$ Justify your answer without graphing on a calculator.</paragraph>
    <exercise number="256" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$f(x) = \frac{x + 1}{x^{2} + 5x + 4},a = -1$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="257" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$f(x) = \frac{x}{x - 2},a = 2$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>Yes, there is a vertical asymptote</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="258" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$f(x) = \left( {x + 2} \right)^{3/2},a = -2$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="259" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$f(x) = \left( {x - 1} \right)^{-1/3},a = 1$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>Yes, there is vertical asymptote</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="260" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$f(x) = 1 + x^{-2/5},a = 1$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <paragraph>For the following exercises, evaluate the limit.</paragraph>
    <exercise number="261" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$\underset{x\rightarrow\infty}{\lim }\frac{1}{3x + 6}$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$0 0$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="262" has_solution="false">
